REM CONFIG.SYS - reference copy of what a fleet DOS-game box should boot with.
REM Deploys to C:\CONFIG.SYS. Boot-critical: back up the existing file and read
REM the result back before trusting it, and remember that a SHELL= line naming
REM a missing interpreter makes the machine unbootable. Recovery is F8 at boot
REM -> "Safe mode command prompt only" (which bypasses CONFIG.SYS) followed by
REM COPY C:\CONFIG.BAK C:\CONFIG.SYS.
REM
REM This file was 0 bytes on .243, so MS-DOS mode came up with no XMS at all
REM and DOS itself sitting in conventional memory. HIMEM plus DOS=HIGH is the
REM minimum a DOS game box wants; it is also what lets the loader put DOS in
REM the HMA and leave the 640K for the game.
REM
REM EMM386 is deliberately NOT loaded: it is the classic cause of a Win98 box
REM that hangs on the way into MS-DOS mode, and nothing here needs EMS. Games
REM that want EMS can get it from a per-game setup later.
REM
REM SHELL= is what sets the MASTER ENVIRONMENT SIZE. Without it DOS uses 256
REM bytes, and .243 already needs ~286: the boot variables (~165) plus
REM AUTOEXEC's BLASTER/MIDI (~47) plus PLAY.BAT's MTCPCFG/DGLOG (~62) plus
REM RUN.BAT's PATH prepend. Past the limit SET fails SILENTLY - %DGLOG%
REM expands to nothing, so the logging that diagnoses everything else turns
REM into "echo ... >>" with no target, and mTCP loses MTCPCFG, which is how
REM DHCP and HTGET find their configuration. 1024 leaves generous headroom.
REM The second argument is the COMSPEC directory; /P makes it permanent.
SHELL=C:\COMMAND.COM C:\ /P /E:1024
DEVICE=C:\WINDOWS\HIMEM.SYS
DOS=HIGH
FILES=40
BUFFERS=20
STACKS=9,256
